  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable to slightly above average temperatures and dry \nconditions through Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure remains centered over the area extending across the \nsoutheast CONUS to offshore New England this afternoon. An upper \nlevel trough over north central Canada will continue pushing the \nhigh pressure further offshore tonight and Thursday, as well as \ndragging a weak cold front across the area Thursday night. \nAdditionally, Tropical Storm Isaias sits in the southwestern Gulf, \nslowly drifting east before making a northern turn. Temperatures \ntoday have moderated back to near normal, sitting in the lower to \nmid 70s, with the exception of the immediate coast and Eastern Shore \nwhich are in the upper 60s. Tonight's lows won't be as chilly as \nlast night, with expected lows in the mid 50s inland and upper 50s \nalong the coast. With the high pressure shifting more offshore \nThursday, temperatures will rise to the upper 70s to lower 80s (mid \n80s inland) ahead of the aforementioned cold front. This cold front \ncrosses the region Thursday night with temperatures trending down to \nnear seasonal averages Friday and Saturday. Dry conditions continue \nthrough Saturday. However, a gradual increase in high levels clouds \nis expected as upper level moisture spreads over the region well in \nadvance of remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture and rain chances return Saturday night\ninto early Monday.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, there is increasing confidence that moisture \ngets pulled northward and then pushed eastward across the region as \nIsaias makes landfall and merges with an upper trough in vicinity of \nthe Mid-South. The 12z/07 global models have came into better \nagreement of rainfall timing showing late Saturday night/early \nSunday morning timeframe. Heavy rain is possible as ample moisture \nwill be present, 12z ensemble guidance continuing to show PW values \nat or above 200% of normal along with a plume of instability surging \nN ahead of the trough. However, there is still considerable spread \nbetween the 10th and 90th percentile QPF in the 12z/07 ensemble \nguidance. In general, the 10th percentile (reasonable low-end) \naccumulated precip through 00z Tuesday is 0.25-0.5\", while the 90th \npercentile is on the order of 1.5-3.0\". The 50th percentile (most \nlikely case) shows a generalized 0.75-1.25\" of rainfall, with the \nhighest rainfall totals in the souther half of the area. PoPs have \nincreased to 90-95% from late Saturday night to early Monday morning \nto account for the increased confidence. Behind this system, drier \nair will filter back in later Monday through the middle of the week. \nTemperatures should remain near average late this weekend through \nthe middle of next week.",
